{"subject":"Anatomy","sourceTitle":"All In One Online","title":"PrepLadder · 34.Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","deckName":"created by tele @Neet_pg_bot::prep::Qbank::Anatomy::34.Anatomy__Pancreas,_Kidney,_and_Ureter","questions":[{"id":"all-in-one-1760436511314","topic":"PrepLadder · 34.Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","number":1,"stem":"What structure crosses the ureter anteriorly on both sides during its abdominal course?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior mesenteric artery"},{"label":"B","text":"Gonadal vessels"},{"label":"C","text":"Genitofemoral nerve"},{"label":"D","text":"Duodenum"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"Gonadal vessels","solutionHtml":"er is <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"6b4030e51a1692777185c6a9dc95b0\">crossed</span> anteriorly by the gonadal vessels during its <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1665a40f1016927771801899eaed5d\">abdominal</span> course.\n<ul>\n<li>These vessels are the testicular <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a66cd3adaa1692777182e77cb23021\">artery</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"75ddd4cc7016927772012e0acd424d\">vein</span> in males and the ovarian <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a66cd3adaa1692777182e77cb23021\">artery</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"75ddd4cc7016927772012e0acd424d\">vein</span> in females.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"4167\" src=\"images/IJX95t8WoIhBPkOoIHmH1740370349.png\"\" width=\"4167\"/></p>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:624px\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Relations of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1665a40f1016927771801899eaed5d\">abdominal</span> part of the Ureter</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\"> </td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Anterior Relation</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Posterior Relation</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Right Ureter</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>The second part of the Duodenum</li>\n<li>Right Colic vessels</li>\n<li>Ileocolic vessels</li>\n<li>Right testicular and ovarian vessels</li>\n<li>Root of mesentery</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1ca45620ed1692777196a47f648338\">Psoas</span> Major</li>\n<li>Bifurcation of Right Common <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"837bfc230c16927771892ece07f94b\">Iliac</span> artery</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Left Ureter</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Left Colic vessels</li>\n<li>Sigmoidal vessels</li>\n<li>Left testicular and ovarian vessels</li>\n<li>Sigmoid mesocolon</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1ca45620ed1692777196a47f648338\">Psoas</span> Major</li>\n<li>Bifurcation of Left Common <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"837bfc230c16927771892ece07f94b\">Iliac</span> artery</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/>\n </p>\n<p><strong>Inferior mesenteric <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a66cd3adaa1692777182e77cb23021\">artery</span> (Option A): </strong>The <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"49c4ef5b9d1692777190c0277f8344\">inferior</span> mesenteric <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a66cd3adaa1692777182e77cb23021\">artery</span> is a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"db46007ad21692777192dcc160a2e2\">medial</span> relation to the left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8cde7563da1692777201fca2b1f090\">ureter</span> but does not cross it anteriorly.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Genitofemoral nerve (Option C): </strong>The <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8cde7563da1692777201fca2b1f090\">ureter</span> crosses <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1ba6a9cfe11692777181950edd7a6d\">anterior</span> to the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"65280a763b1692777188eb28595e8b\">genitofemoral</span> nerve as it travels down the abdomen, making it a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a5c6cb079d16927771967e62ffee72\">posterior</span> relation.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Duodenum (Option D): </strong>The <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0cf8ac70611692777186b133d027f6\">descending</span> part of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"280e59e9331692777186f3beac2c41\">duodenum</span> only crosses the right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8cde7563da1692777201fca2b1f090\">ureter</span> anteriorly at its origin near the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> pelvis, but it does not cross the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8cde7563da1692777201fca2b1f090\">ureter</span> on both sides.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8cde7563da1692777201fca2b1f090\">ureter</span> is not related to the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"280e59e9331692777186f3beac2c41\">duodenum</span> at all.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p><br/><img src=\"images/IJX95t8WoIhBPkOoIHmH1740370349.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Gray’s Anatomy, 42nd Edition, Page 1273<br/>Netter Atlas of Human Anatomy, 8th Edition, Plate 335, Page 463<br/>Textbook of Anatomy Vishram Singh, 2nd Edition, Volume 2, Page 178</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436511316","topic":"PrepLadder · 34.Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","number":2,"stem":"Which vessels primarily supply the middle part of the ureter?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal artery"},{"label":"B","text":"Vesical vessels"},{"label":"C","text":"Aorta"},{"label":"D","text":"Uterine vessels"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Aorta","solutionHtml":"he middle part of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8cde7563da1692777201fca2b1f090\">ureter</span> is primarily supplied by branches from the aorta.\n<li>It can also receive blood from gonadal or <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"837bfc230c16927771892ece07f94b\">iliac</span> vessels, but the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ce68eab48a1692777181c719b4344e\">aorta</span> is the key vessel for this segment.</li>\n\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong><u>Blood Supply of the Ureter</u></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arteries are closely associated with the peritoneum.</li>\n<li>They <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a1b06f401f1692777203a53393ba98\">form</span> a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3a9085a5761692777195417d7c03ae\">plexus</span> on the ureter's surface with <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"62f0d7c8491692777182ab7cc82edb\">ascending</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0cf8ac70611692777186b133d027f6\">descending</span> branches.</li>\n<li>The upper and middle parts receive blood from the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"db46007ad21692777192dcc160a2e2\">medial</span> side, while the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7cd6eb19341692777195d83aafdcb3\">pelvic</span> part is supplied from the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc380e21321692777191739bd99191\">lateral</span> side.</li>\n<li><strong>Upper Part:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Supplied by branches from the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> artery. <strong>(Option A ruled out)</strong></li>\n<li>May also receive branches from gonadal or colic vessels.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Middle Part:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Receives branches from the aorta.</li>\n<li>Can also receive branches from gonadal or <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"837bfc230c16927771892ece07f94b\">iliac</span> vessels.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pelvic Part:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Supplied by branches from vesical, middle rectal, or <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"59ff8b38191692777201150e72ebc3\">uterine</span> vessels. <strong>(Options B &amp; C ruled out)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"> </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Fig: </strong><strong>Blood Supply of the Ureter</strong></p>\n<ul>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"500\" src=\"images/x5SC8JHQa6gqnWjq5Qgd1740370578.png\"\" width=\"500\"/></p><br/><img src=\"images/x5SC8JHQa6gqnWjq5Qgd1740370578.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Gray’s Anatomy, 42nd edition, Page 1273<br/>BD Chaurasia’s Human Anatomy, Volume 2, 8th edition, Pages 377, 378<br/>Snells Clinical Anatomy, 10th edition, Page 384</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436511318","topic":"PrepLadder · 34.Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","number":3,"stem":"A 9 y/o child presents with abdominal pain and hematuria. Imaging reveals that the left renal vein is compressed between the abdominal aorta and the superior mesenteric artery. What is a key anatomical feature contributing to this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The left renal vein passes behind the abdominal aorta."},{"label":"B","text":"The pancreas lies directly posterior to the superior mesenteric artery."},{"label":"C","text":"The left renal vein travels behind the pancreas before crossing the aorta."},{"label":"D","text":"The pancreas is situated inferior to the left renal vein."}],"answer":"C","answerText":"The left renal vein travels behind the pancreas before crossing the aorta.","solutionHtml":"-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\"&amp;gt;renal vein's route behind the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2e2fe8c6c116927771946fb9a8adb0\">pancreas</span> contributes to its <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"6ced8252211692777199ab0cc3f2bb\">susceptibility</span> to compression.\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong><u>Relationship of the Pancreas, Veins, and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ce68eab48a1692777181c719b4344e\">Aorta</span> in Nutcracker Syndrome</u></strong></p>\n<p><strong>Nutcracker Syndrome: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A condition caused by <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"56af81fad316927771843d2b4b1e4f\">compression</span> of the left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"75ddd4cc7016927772012e0acd424d\">vein</span> leading to the left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"75ddd4cc7016927772012e0acd424d\">vein</span> hypertension.</li>\n<li>Highlights the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"116f86834a1692777181a914379270\">anatomical</span> relationship between the pancreas, veins, and aorta.</li>\n</ul>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:614px\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Anatomical Features</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Description</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">Renal</span> Vein</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Originates from the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4fe753e1a91692777189b7bdb3e2e1\">hilum</span> of the left kidney.</p>\n<p>Travels behind the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2e2fe8c6c116927771946fb9a8adb0\">pancreas</span> and splenic vein. <strong>(Option C) </strong><strong>(Option D ruled out)</strong></p>\n<p>Crosses the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1665a40f1016927771801899eaed5d\">abdominal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ce68eab48a1692777181c719b4344e\">aorta</span> anteriorly below the superior mesenteric artery. <strong>(Option A ruled out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Nutcracker Analogy</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>The left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"75ddd4cc7016927772012e0acd424d\">vein</span> is compressed between the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ce68eab48a1692777181c719b4344e\">aorta</span> and superior mesenteric artery, resembling a nutcracker.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Symptoms</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Can lead to left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"75ddd4cc7016927772012e0acd424d\">vein</span> hypertension.</p>\n<p>Symptoms include <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"660645b0c91692777188bbdf3fedff\">hematuria</span> and varicocele, especially in children.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pancreas' Role in Nutcracker Syndrome: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Positioned directly in front of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"56af81fad316927771843d2b4b1e4f\">compression</span> site, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1ba6a9cfe11692777181950edd7a6d\">anterior</span> to the SMA. <strong>(Option B ruled out)</strong></li>\n<li>Proximity to <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"526ede3fee169277720184378acc5e\">vascular</span> structures suggests the potential to worsen the condition.</li>\n<li>The <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"de89ce6b2616927772011395881cae\">uncinate</span> process being close to the superior mesenteric <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a66cd3adaa1692777182e77cb23021\">artery</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"75ddd4cc7016927772012e0acd424d\">vein</span> further emphasises the pancreas's proximity to major vessels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Gray&rsquo;s Anatomy, 42nd Edition, Pages 1123, 1140, 1227, 1267</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436511320","topic":"PrepLadder · 34.Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","number":4,"stem":"Which vein receives drainage from both the left gonadal and left adrenal veins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior vena cava"},{"label":"B","text":"Right renal vein"},{"label":"C","text":"Left renal vein"},{"label":"D","text":"Right adrenal vein"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Left renal vein","solutionHtml":"nd left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f955f012f716927771801d01e7d163\">adrenal</span> veins drain into the left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> vein, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"397f16a7a71692777187ffffefa7e6\">emptying</span> into the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"49c4ef5b9d1692777190c0277f8344\">inferior</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3ddf6d5ea71692777201b70c8b9715\">vena</span> cava.\n<ul>\n<li>This is a key <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"116f86834a1692777181a914379270\">anatomical</span> difference from the right side, where the veins drain directly into the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"49c4ef5b9d1692777190c0277f8344\">inferior</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3ddf6d5ea71692777201b70c8b9715\">vena</span> cava.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"4167\" src=\"images/CVmTNuNAUcvsORzJvQmH1740370057.png\"\" width=\"4167\"/></p>\n<p><strong>Inferior <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3ddf6d5ea71692777201b70c8b9715\">vena</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"67544ba6ef16927771835d4641cc8f\">cava</span> (Option A): </strong>While the right gonadal and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f955f012f716927771801d01e7d163\">adrenal</span> veins drain directly into the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"49c4ef5b9d1692777190c0277f8344\">inferior</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3ddf6d5ea71692777201b70c8b9715\">vena</span> cava, the left gonadal and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f955f012f716927771801d01e7d163\">adrenal</span> veins drain into the left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"75ddd4cc7016927772012e0acd424d\">vein</span> instead.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"75ddd4cc7016927772012e0acd424d\">vein</span> (Option B): </strong>The right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"75ddd4cc7016927772012e0acd424d\">vein</span> handles <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"dd824a31e71692777186c7f8168998\">drainage</span> from structures on the right side of the body, such as the right kidney.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It does not receive blood from the left gonadal or <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f955f012f716927771801d01e7d163\">adrenal</span> veins.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f955f012f716927771801d01e7d163\">adrenal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"75ddd4cc7016927772012e0acd424d\">vein</span> (Option D): </strong>The right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f955f012f716927771801d01e7d163\">adrenal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"75ddd4cc7016927772012e0acd424d\">vein</span> is responsible for draining blood from the right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f955f012f716927771801d01e7d163\">adrenal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4744bb90c21692777188c8957f9faa\">gland</span> into the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"49c4ef5b9d1692777190c0277f8344\">inferior</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3ddf6d5ea71692777201b70c8b9715\">vena</span> cava.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It does not interact with the left gonadal or <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f955f012f716927771801d01e7d163\">adrenal</span> veins.<strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p><br/><img src=\"images/CVmTNuNAUcvsORzJvQmH1740370057.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Gray’s Anatomy, 42nd Edition, Page 1267<br/>Netter Atlas of Human Anatomy, 8th Edition, Plate 332, Page 460<br/>Textbook of Anatomy Vishram Singh, 2nd Edition, Volume 2, Pages 194,69</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436511322","topic":"PrepLadder · 34.Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","number":5,"stem":"Which of the following layers immediately surrounds the kidney and adheres closely to its surface?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pararenal fat"},{"label":"B","text":"Fibrous capsule"},{"label":"C","text":"Renal fascia"},{"label":"D","text":"Perirenal fat"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"Fibrous capsule","solutionHtml":"eta\" data-dictid=\"5d6dfa73b316927772028f3cd60f7f\"&amp;gt;fibrous capsule, also known as the true capsule, is a thin, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5d6dfa73b316927772028f3cd60f7f\">fibrous</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"34cd707428169277719272942b7905\">membrane</span> that tightly adheres to the surface of the kidney.\n<ul>\n<li>It is the kidney's immediate protective layer, safeguarding it from minor injuries.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Internal Structure</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><u>Cortex: </u>Outer region, extends between <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> pyramids as <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> columns.</li>\n<li><u>Medulla</u>: Contains 5-11 <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> pyramids, whose <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"37e7c553a216927771814a90e4edcd\">apices</span> (renal papillae) project into minor calyces.</li>\n<li><u>Renal sinus:</u> Houses <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> pelvis, calyces, vessels, and fat.</li>\n<li><u>Calyces</u>: Minor <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b8daf4c798169277718363179ec885\">calyces</span> collect urine, merging into major <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b8daf4c798169277718363179ec885\">calyces</span> that <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a1b06f401f1692777203a53393ba98\">form</span> the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> pelvis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Microscopic Structure</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><u>Nephron:</u> Functional unit responsible for filtering blood and producing urine, consisting of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7c014f0a121692777185a3c89b4e6a\">corpuscle</span> and tubule.</li>\n<li><u>Collecting ducts</u>: Drain <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0096f2a7631692777201eb9f21d5a7\">urine</span> from nephrons into <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"62b9af909d1692777194cd004f10f4\">papillary</span> ducts, which lead to the minor calyces.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"BruceBlaus.\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"CC BY 3.0\" data-source=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blausen_0592_KidneyAnatomy_01.png\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"4167\" src=\"images/tlYfMLakZHJNuaETTFJ81740370128.png\"\" width=\"4167\"/></p>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:624px\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Coverings of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4aa6035df41692777190b41d4a1583\">kidney</span> (From inside out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Description</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Function</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:middle\">\n<p><strong>Renal Capsule</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(Option B)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>A thin, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5d6dfa73b316927772028f3cd60f7f\">fibrous</span> layer directly covering the kidney. Contains mesenchymal stem cells (MSCs).</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Protection, shape maintenance, stem cell <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5577d95538169277719314fcd0c6c6\">niche</span> for repair</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:middle\">\n<p><strong>Perirenal Fat</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(Option D ruled out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Adipose tissue surrounds the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"54635b1bf01692777183247ecff5e0\">capsule</span> (also called <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"419338f1b71692777195411fd0bfcd\">perinephric</span> fat).</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Cushions and insulates the kidney</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:middle\">\n<p><strong>Renal Fascia</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(Option C ruled out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Connective tissue (Gerota's fascia) around <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"07eb540b841692777195078fd4c928\">perirenal</span> fat, anchoring the kidney.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Secures <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4aa6035df41692777190b41d4a1583\">kidney</span> position and separates from adjacent organs</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:middle\">\n<p><strong>Pararenal Fat </strong><strong>(Option A ruled out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Outer <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5c2a4a949d16927771802ba54640cc\">adipose</span> layer beyond the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> fascia.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Provides additional cushioning and support</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/>\n<br/>\n </p>\n<p> </p><br/><img src=\"images/tlYfMLakZHJNuaETTFJ81740370128.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Gray’s Anatomy, 42nd Edition, Page 1260<br/>Netter Atlas of Human Anatomy, 8th Edition, Plate 333, Page 461<br/>Textbook of Anatomy Vishram Singh, 2nd Edition, Volume 2, Pages 168,169</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436511324","topic":"PrepLadder · 34.Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","number":6,"stem":"Which structure is in direct contact with the upper pole of the right kidney?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right lobe of the liver"},{"label":"B","text":"Duodenum"},{"label":"C","text":"Right suprarenal gland"},{"label":"D","text":"Right colic flexure"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Right suprarenal gland","solutionHtml":"=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9b77587bb116927771990fc50f3904\"&amp;gt;suprarenal <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4744bb90c21692777188c8957f9faa\">gland</span> lies directly on the upper pole of the right kidney, potentially overlapping the kidney's <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"db46007ad21692777192dcc160a2e2\">medial</span> border.\n<ul>\n<li>There is no intervening peritoneum, making the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4744bb90c21692777188c8957f9faa\">gland</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4aa6035df41692777190b41d4a1583\">kidney</span> directly connected.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:NaN\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Anterior Relations of Kidney</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Right Kidney</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Left Kidney</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>The right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d7dd6ae43c1692777191cf75c0bd58\">lobe</span> of the Liver</li>\n<li>Right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9b77587bb116927771990fc50f3904\">suprarenal</span> gland</li>\n<li>The second part of the duodenum</li>\n<li>Jejunum</li>\n<li>Hepatic (Right colic) flexure</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9b77587bb116927771990fc50f3904\">suprarenal</span> gland</li>\n<li>Stomach</li>\n<li>Spleen</li>\n<li>Jejunum</li>\n<li>Left colic flexure</li>\n<li>Pancreas and splenic vessels</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"4167\" src=\"images/Db98FUrLa9O1NkMeQn1C1740370213.png\"\" width=\"4167\"/></p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"4167\" src=\"images/JdS8TdhxFj90Fnlo5BKM1740370235.png\"\" width=\"4167\"/></p>\n<p><strong>The right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d7dd6ae43c1692777191cf75c0bd58\">lobe</span> of the liver (Option A): </strong>While the right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d7dd6ae43c1692777191cf75c0bd58\">lobe</span> of the liver covers a large portion of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1ba6a9cfe11692777181950edd7a6d\">anterior</span> surface of the right kidney, they <strong>are separated by a layer of peritoneum</strong><strong>. </strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Duodenum (Option B): </strong>The <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0cf8ac70611692777186b133d027f6\">descending</span> part of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"280e59e9331692777186f3beac2c41\">duodenum</span> lies against the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"db46007ad21692777192dcc160a2e2\">medial</span> side of the right kidney.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>However, it contacts the central portion of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4aa6035df41692777190b41d4a1583\">kidney</span> rather than the upper pole.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Right colic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"13e47cfa68169277720299cd54beb4\">flexure</span> (Option D): </strong>The right colic flexure, the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9f0942c0b51692777190ee876713e5\">junction</span> of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"62f0d7c8491692777182ab7cc82edb\">ascending</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a6260fa86416927772008d36bd7e5f\">transverse</span> colon, is located <strong>inferiorly</strong> and laterally to the right kidney.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p><br/><img src=\"images/Db98FUrLa9O1NkMeQn1C1740370213.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/JdS8TdhxFj90Fnlo5BKM1740370235.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Gray’s Anatomy, 42nd Edition, Page 1261<br/>Netter Atlas of Human Anatomy, 8th Edition, Plate 330, Page 458<br/>Textbook of Anatomy Vishram Singh, 2nd Edition, Volume 2, Pages 166,167</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436511326","topic":"PrepLadder · 34.Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","number":7,"stem":"Which rib is related to the posterior surface of the left kidney but not the right kidney?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7th rib"},{"label":"B","text":"8th rib"},{"label":"C","text":"12th rib"},{"label":"D","text":"11th rib"}],"answer":"D","answerText":"11th rib","solutionHtml":"ictid=\"a5c6cb079d16927771967e62ffee72\"&amp;gt;posterior relation of the left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4aa6035df41692777190b41d4a1583\">kidney</span> but not the right kidney.\n<ul>\n<li>The right <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4aa6035df41692777190b41d4a1583\">kidney</span> is related to the 12th rib, while the left <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4aa6035df41692777190b41d4a1583\">kidney</span> is related to both the 11th and 12th ribs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:NaN\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Posterior Relations of Kidney</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Right Kidney</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Left Kidney</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Only 12th Rib</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>11th Rib</li>\n<li>12th Rib</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>( Common to both Right and Left Kidney)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><u>Muscles</u>: <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5b42867b61169277718692205c2447\">Diaphragm</span> and in <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5ad207273316927771927cdef8f83d\">mediolateral</span> order - <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1ca45620ed1692777196a47f648338\">Psoas</span> Major, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2443adae9d16927771966d5111bc18\">Quadratus</span> Lumborum, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b4b7ff709c1692777200a120b1b991\">Transversus</span> Abdominis</li>\n<li><u>Ligaments</u>: <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"db46007ad21692777192dcc160a2e2\">Medial</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc380e21321692777191739bd99191\">Lateral</span> Arcuate</li>\n<li><u>Nerves</u>: Subcostal, Iliohypogastric, Ilioinguinal</li>\n<li><u>Vessels</u>: Subcostal</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"4167\" src=\"images/tC5KG9t6KcyNkXsllHZh1740370291.png\"\" width=\"4167\"/></p>\n<p><strong>7th rib (Option A): </strong>The 7th rib is located higher in the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3c6021096816927772007954fcc158\">thoracic</span> region and does not have any direct relation to the kidneys.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>8th rib (Option B): </strong>The 8th rib is positioned too high to be a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a5c6cb079d16927771967e62ffee72\">posterior</span> relation to either kidney.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>12th rib (Option C): </strong>The 12th rib is related to both the right and left kidneys, not just the left kidney. It lies posteriorly to both kidneys.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p><br/><img src=\"images/tC5KG9t6KcyNkXsllHZh1740370291.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Gray’s Anatomy, 42nd Edition, Page 1261<br/>Netter Atlas of Human Anatomy, 8th Edition, Plate 331, Page 459<br/>Textbook of Anatomy Vishram Singh, 2nd Edition, Volume 2, Pages 167,168</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436511328","topic":"PrepLadder · 34.Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","number":8,"stem":"Which of the following is not a typical site of ureteric constriction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Junction with the renal pelvis"},{"label":"B","text":"Crossing by ductus deferens"},{"label":"C","text":"At the pelvic outlet"},{"label":"D","text":"Opening at the trigone"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"At the pelvic outlet","solutionHtml":"77195d83aafdcb3\"&amp;gt;pelvic outlet is not typically recognized as a site of ureteric constriction.\n<ul>\n<li>Ureteric <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4880e9b3761692777185e64eba797c\">constriction</span> occurs at the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7cd6eb19341692777195d83aafdcb3\">pelvic</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"42380fc2fb169277719066eff35739\">inlet</span> ( <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7cd6eb19341692777195d83aafdcb3\">pelvic</span> brim) where it crosses the common <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"837bfc230c16927771892ece07f94b\">iliac</span> artery.</li>\n</ul>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:624px\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong><u>Ureteric constrictions</u></strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>The ureters have several points of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4880e9b3761692777185e64eba797c\">constriction</span> that are clinically significant, particularly for <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> stone impaction. The typical sites of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4880e9b3761692777185e64eba797c\">constriction</span> are:</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>1. <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9f0942c0b51692777190ee876713e5\">Junction</span> with the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> pelvis</strong>: This is where the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8cde7563da1692777201fca2b1f090\">ureter</span> narrows as it transitions from the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b9356afdba16927771979d068c6587\">renal</span> pelvis. <strong>(Option A ruled out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"5\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"4167\" src=\"images/5KK3LTVfjH20Fui90sJe1740370532.png\"\" width=\"4167\"/></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>2. At the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ad3a6758111692777191fa157f6ece\">linea</span> terminalis</strong>: Near the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"db46007ad21692777192dcc160a2e2\">medial</span> border of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1ca45620ed1692777196a47f648338\">psoas</span> major, where the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8cde7563da1692777201fca2b1f090\">ureter</span> is kinked as it crosses the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7cd6eb19341692777195d83aafdcb3\">pelvic</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"6e7d00e7451692777183a41163f256\">brim</span> to enter the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c25071fa3816927771955eeaf9d45e\">pelvis</span> at the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7cd6eb19341692777195d83aafdcb3\">pelvic</span> inlet.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>3. <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5a2c3ec4ab1692777190af5b350865\">Intramural</span> segment</strong>: As the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8cde7563da1692777201fca2b1f090\">ureter</span> pierces the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e6cfda323816927771839cc83e376d\">bladder</span> wall, it runs obliquely through the wall, which is the <strong>narrowest part</strong>.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>4. Crossing by <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7a6864e97b169277718602072817ee\">ductus</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c8e17879f71692777185360caf5372\">deferens</span> or broad ligament: </strong>The <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8cde7563da1692777201fca2b1f090\">ureter</span> crosses underneath the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7a6864e97b169277718602072817ee\">ductus</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c8e17879f71692777185360caf5372\">deferens</span> in males and lies behind the broad <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"301e3b01f01692777191aced6dc342\">ligament</span> in females. <strong>(Option B ruled out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>5. Opening at the trigone: </strong>The <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8cde7563da1692777201fca2b1f090\">ureter</span> opens at the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc380e21321692777191739bd99191\">lateral</span> angle of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d89806ebbe1692777200b7b677e616\">trigone</span> area within the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0b72f5e96b16927772018dec8a87e2\">urinary</span> bladder. <strong>(Option D ruled out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/>\n </p>\n<p> </p><br/><img src=\"images/5KK3LTVfjH20Fui90sJe1740370532.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Gray’s Anatomy, 42nd edition, Page 1273<br/>BD Chaurasia’s Human Anatomy, Volume 2, 8th edition, Page 375<br/>Snells Clinical Anatomy, 10th edition, Page 449</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436511330","topic":"PrepLadder · 34.Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","number":9,"stem":"Which part of the pancreas is covered by the peritoneum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Head of the pancreas"},{"label":"B","text":"Body of the pancreas"},{"label":"C","text":"Tail of the pancreas"},{"label":"D","text":"Entire pancreas"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Tail of the pancreas","solutionHtml":"<p>The<strong> tail of the pancreas</strong> is the only part of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2e2fe8c6c116927771946fb9a8adb0\">pancreas</span> that is covered by the peritoneum.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It extends towards the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"6a4861c8d71692777199aeb1d55c85\">spleen</span> and is enclosed by the peritoneum, making it intraperitoneal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Retroperitoneal organs: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kidneys</li>\n<li>Duodenum</li>\n<li>Ascending and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0cf8ac70611692777186b133d027f6\">descending</span> colon</li>\n<li>Rectum (lower ⅔ )</li>\n<li>Adrenal glands</li>\n<li>Ureters</li>\n<li>Head and body of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2e2fe8c6c116927771946fb9a8adb0\">Pancreas</span> <strong>(Options A, B)</strong></li>\n<li>Aorta and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"49c4ef5b9d1692777190c0277f8344\">Inferior</span> venacava</li>\n</ul>\n<p><br/>\n </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Textbook of Anatomy Vishram Singh, 2nd Edition, Volume 2, Page 132.</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436511332","topic":"PrepLadder · 34.Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","number":10,"stem":"A 45-year-old male presents with complaints of a progressively enlarged abdomen and discomfort on the left side. Imaging studies reveal splenomegaly. Which structure is primarily responsible for preventing the downward displacement of the spleen in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phrenicocolic Ligament"},{"label":"B","text":"Gastrosplenic Ligament"},{"label":"C","text":"Splenorenal Ligament"},{"label":"D","text":"Diaphragm"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Phrenicocolic Ligament","solutionHtml":"data-dictid=\"5a50cd774d1692777186c56ec68eac\"&amp;gt;displacement of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3dc75b5551169277718748ea352daa\">enlarged spleen</span> is prevented by th<strong>e phrenicolic ligament.</strong>\n<li>The phrenicolic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"301e3b01f01692777191aced6dc342\">ligament</span> connects the splenic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"13e47cfa68169277720299cd54beb4\">flexure</span> of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0fce15e71f1692777184feb9af5303\">colon</span> to the diaphragm. It is not attached to the spleen.</li>\n<li>However, it lies below the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"6a4861c8d71692777199aeb1d55c85\">spleen</span> and supports its lower pole.</li>\n<li>When the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"6a4861c8d71692777199aeb1d55c85\">spleen</span> gets enlarged, phrenicolic ligaments along with the splenic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"13e47cfa68169277720299cd54beb4\">flexure</span> of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0fce15e71f1692777184feb9af5303\">colon</span> restrict its downward displacement.</li>\n<li>Hence, it is also called <strong>Sustentaculum lienis. </strong><br/>\n\t </li>\n\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Ligaments of the spleen: </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"500\" src=\"images/GcEnentsa7mX1q8yvDtE1740370674.png\"\" width=\"500\"/></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p><br/><img src=\"images/GcEnentsa7mX1q8yvDtE1740370674.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Textbook of Anatomy Vishram Singh, 2nd Edition, Volume 2, Pages 104, 105.</div>","tags":""}]}